Cross-modal semantic priming with partial auditory primes seems a good technique to assess spoken-word recognition, because it allows tracking the activation of multiple word&#13;
candidates. However, previous research using this technique has found inconsistent results. First, a priming experiment is reported that addresses this technique’s validity. Results show that semantic priming is not observed with partial auditory primes, but only with full primes.&#13;
Secondly, Monte Carlo simulations are reported of a previous study that found partial priming effects; the simulations show that the particular design in that study yields a high risk of a Type-I error. In conclusion, the semantic priming technique cannot be used to investigate&#13;
activation of multiple word candidates, and its use for that purpose should be discontinued.

